 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "GRANGE STONE CIRCLE. The largest stone circle in Ireland, raised about four thousand years ago, more than a hundred stones set edge to edge round a level floor. At the summer solstice the sun rises in line with the entrance.",
   "plaque": "limerick_grange_stone_circle",
   "title": "Grange Stone Circle"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "LOUGH GUR. People have lived round this lake for six thousand years. The remains of Neolithic houses, crann\u00f3gs, forts and tombs crowd its shores, which makes it one of the richest archaeological places in Ireland.",
   "plaque": "limerick_lough_gur",
   "title": "Lough Gur"
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "GEAR\u00d3ID IARLA. Gerald FitzGerald, Earl of Desmond, was a fourteenth-century poet in Irish and, they said, a magician. Legend says he sleeps under Lough Gur and rides out over the water every seven years on a horse shod with silver. When the shoes wear through, he will return.",
   "plaque": "limerick_gearoid_iarla",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Gear\u00f3id Iarla"
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "THE LOUGH GUR SHIELD. A Bronze Age shield of beaten bronze, about 2,700 years old, found near the lake. Its rings of studs still shine. It's in the National Museum in Dublin now; the lake would like it back.",
   "plaque": "limerick_lough_gur_shield",
   "title": "The Lough Gur Shield"
  }
 ],
